The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says there is one issue and one queued
fix. The report export and the cause logic show two different problems. Keep a separate
engineering ticket for each, and do not treat the intake tag as proof of the cause.

- [Shared parser reads day-first dates month first](https://github.com/tauzen/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
- [Booking-v3 displays the wrong date hint](https://github.com/tauzen/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)

## What the desk merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table deduplicates the export by report ID and uses the tenant's locale, booking form,
and report channel. In day-first locales the shared parser reads dates month first. Separately,
booking-v3 displays a month-first hint even though that form reads dates in the tenant's
day-first order. Fixing one does not fix the other.

The table also marks reports that desk notes describe as one-day moves. A month/day swap
does not by itself explain those descriptions; investigate the original reports before
assuming the same cause or assigning them to a separate bug. The table's counts still follow
the exported cause rules, so those reports are included in their displayed groups.

## When reports arrived and were merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The desk continued receiving reports after the bulk merge. A merged ticket is not evidence
that the problem stopped, and a queued fix is not evidence that it shipped.

## Triage from here

- Group by the actual cause, not the subject or browser intake tag. The accepted
  [subject-only decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) can merge different bugs
  and split reports of the same bug; do not silently edit that decision record.
- Check the tenant's locale and form, the channel, and the reported dates. Escalate
  ambiguous one-day changes rather than inferring a timezone cause without evidence.
- Keep the two engineering issues and their counts alongside the source report IDs. Update
  counts when the desk receives more reports; this page's components reflect their input data.

